/**
 * Polish, kept out of the pack files — the second table written to the
 * table-per-language layer described in lib/translations.ts, and the second
 * one a whole course is taught from.
 *
 * WHY A TABLE AND NOT A COLUMN. The packs hold 21,000-odd entries written as
 * `{ de, en }`. French was bolted on as a third field `fr:` before the table
 * layer existed, and doing that again for Polish would mean editing megabytes
 * of pack files for a diff nobody could read. Keyed by the German instead,
 * this file is the only thing that had to be written, and no existing entry
 * changed — so nothing that already worked could break by being edited.
 *
 * WHAT MAKES IT A COURSE RATHER THAN A GLOSS TABLE. Polish is not shown beside
 * the German; it REPLACES it on the target row for a learner whose course is
 * Polish (see polishCourse.ts, which narrows every pack to what this file
 * covers). A card is only taught in Polish if it is in here, so what is
 * written below is the syllabus, not a decoration.
 *
 * THE STANDARD. Same as frenchTranslations.ts: what a Polish speaker would
 * actually say in that situation, at the register the German uses.
 *   - Übung macht den Meister → Ćwiczenie czyni mistrza.
 *   - Ich versteh nur Bahnhof → To dla mnie czarna magia. (the idiom, not the
 *     train station)
 *   - Sie hat ihm einen Korb gegeben → Dała mu kosza. Polish happens to share
 *     that picture; where it does not, the Polish saying that does the same
 *     job wins over a literal rendering nobody says.
 *   - Spoken Polish drops the pronoun: "Idę do domu.", never "Ja idę do domu."
 * A literal translation no Pole would say is worse than none, because the
 * learner cannot tell the difference.
 *
 * NOUNS ARE KEYED BARE. translate() strips a leading der/die/das before its
 * second lookup, so "Haus" answers for "das Haus". Polish has no articles, so
 * there is nothing the German article adds and everything to gain from one
 * key per noun.
 *
 * FORMAL ADDRESS IS WRITTEN WITH Pan. Polish says Pan to a man and Pani to a
 * woman, and a card can only show one. The masculine form is written out and
 * the swap is the same word either way, which is a smaller lie than a slash
 * the matcher would read as two words.
 *
 * WHERE TWO GERMAN CARDS MEET ONE POLISH WORD — "Guten Morgen" and "Guten
 * Tag" are both "Dzień dobry" — they are deliberately left to collide, and
 * polishPart() keeps one card carrying both meanings. That is the right
 * lesson: Polish really does have one word there.
 */

